Z||000004||mshill||06-19-2000||01:15 PM||||David,  with regards to the 87 6 cylinder...  If the engine was warmed up, how come it was still in open loop mode?  I see the coolant temperature was 200F, but it seems it should be in closed loop node at that point. Just curious.||||reg||1
Z||000005||David Gamache||06-19-2000||06:00 PM||||The first test said closed loop the next test a few minutes later said open. This is not the only reading that changed. The vehicle runs fine so i have contacted auto Xray to ask a few questions and they are supposed to get back to me soon. On one vehicle it says the brake is on and it is not. The basics such as the temp and volts appear correct.||||reg||1
Z||000006||BN Boomer||06-19-2000||06:50 PM||||Did you correctly configure your scanner each time before changing back and forth between vehicles?||||reg||1
Z||000007||David Gamache||06-20-2000||09:02 PM||||The scanner was configured properly.  When I contacted the manufacturer, although the scanner was brand new, the software program was old.  So it is being returned to the manufacturer for an upgrade.  If anyone has purchased a new scanner they may wish to contact Tech Assistance at Auto Xray to make sure their software is the most updated version.  I will post new results when I receive the unit back from service.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||06-20-2000||10:28 PM||||The 87+ 4 cyl ECM has the cruise module integrated into the ECM wether the car uses it or not. If the car doesn't have the cruise options the brake on indication will always be set. This locks down the cruise sub system so it can't ever activate with random values.<P>Most of the numbers you get will always vary from someone elses. No two motors are exactly alike.<P>Other data is only usesfull when you are doing certain tests. For example you'd only use IAC motor status when you are checking the wiring to the IAC. If it says one or the other winding is powered, but there is no power at the IAC plug, you have a wire or ECM problem, not nesisarily a bad IAC.<P>On that V6, there is no reason the mode should ever be open loop with the engine at 200F unless something is going on. check the O2 sensor wires. replace the O2 sensor if it is damaged in any way.<P>Other numbers in your lists are Very dynamic. The MAT number on the 4 can change 40-50F in seconds. (The V6 one is located diferantly and doesn't change so much or so fast.) If you are having any odd problems with the 4 replace the MAT. It's prone to getting fried by engine backfires. Use only plastic caged MAT's from someone like Borg Warner. (This version of the sensor responds faster. you can use plastic or metal caged MATs on the V6. The metal is a little better for the V6. you won't bash it with an air filter change.)[[Unlike the V6 the 4 cyl MAT plays a big role in engine performance, and will cause some fairly bizare things if it's been cooked. This sensor should be reomved for inspection anytime the engine has been backfired thru the intake.]] ||||reg||1
